Rule.fixtext:
Limit the number of connections allowed by a specific user. 

As the DBA (root), log in to the database (mariadb -u root -p) and run: 

GRANT USAGE ON *.* TO  'username'@'server'  WITH MAX_USER_CONNECTIONS  number of connections;

check.check-content:
As the DBA, check the privileges of all users and roles in the database.

Find all users:

MariaDB> SELECT user, host FROM mysql.user WHERE is_role = 'N';

Find all roles:

MariaDB> SELECT user FROM mysql.user WHERE is_role = 'Y';

For each user found, check grants:

MariaDB> SHOW GRANTS FOR 'username'@'host';

For each role found, check grants: 

MariaDB> SHOW GRANTS FOR 'rolename';

Review all users and roles and their associated privileges. If any users and/or roles privileges exceed those documented, this is a finding.

As the DBA, check the configured authentication settings:

MariaDB> SHOW PLUGINS;

Find users not using PAM plugin for authentication: 

MariaDB> SELECT user, host, plugin FROM mysql.user WHERE plugin != 'pam';

If any users are returned, this is a finding. 

Review all entries and their associated authentication methods. If any entries do not have their documented authentication requirements, this is a finding.

Rule.fixtext:
Grant the necessary privileges to authorized users:

MariaDB> GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON mysql.server_audit_filters TO 'username'@'host';
MariaDB> GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON mysql.server_audit_users TO 'username'@'host';

For users found with access who are not authorized to modify audit filters, review the user's privileges and update accordingly.

check.check-content:
Verify audit tables exist:
SHOW TABLES FROM mysql LIKE 'server_audit%';
The expected results are "server_audit_filters" and "server_audit_users".
If "server_audit_filters" and "server_audit_users" are missing, this is a finding.
Identify users with global privileges that could modify audit configuration:
SELECT user, host, Insert_priv, Update_priv, Delete_priv, Drop_priv, Super_priv FROM mysql.user WHERE Insert_priv='Y' OR Update_priv='Y' OR Delete_priv='Y' OR Drop_priv='Y' OR Super_priv='Y';
If any non-ISSM-approved users have these privileges, this is a finding.
Identify users with privileges on the mysql database:
SELECT user, host, db, Insert_priv, Update_priv, Delete_priv, Drop_priv FROM mysql.db WHERE db='mysql' AND (Insert_priv='Y' OR Update_priv='Y' OR Delete_priv='Y' OR Drop_priv='Y');
If any non-ISSM-approved users have these privileges, this is a finding.
Identify users with table-level privileges on audit tables:
SELECT user, host, db, table_name, table_priv FROM mysql.tables_priv WHERE db='mysql' AND table_name IN ('server_audit_filters','server_audit_users') AND (table_priv LIKE '%Insert%' OR table_priv LIKE '%Update%' OR table_priv LIKE '%Delete%' OR table_priv LIKE '%Drop%');
If any non-ISSM-approved users have these privileges, this is a finding.
For each user and role identified above, verify effective privileges:
SHOW GRANTS FOR 'username'@'host'; SELECT user FROM mysql.user WHERE is_role='Y'; SHOW GRANTS FOR 'rolename';
If any user or role not designated by the ISSM has privileges allowing modification of "mysql.server_audit_filters", "mysql.server_audit_users", the mysql database, or audit-related global settings, this is a finding.

Rule.fixtext:
As the DBA, revoke any permissions from a role that are deemed unnecessary by running the following SQL:

REVOKE privilege_type ON database_name.table_name FROM 'username'@'host';

Revoke any roles from a user if they are deemed unnecessary by running the following SQL:

REVOKE  ROLE_NAME  FROM  test_user ;

If the revoked role is the default role for the user, the "REVOKE" command should be followed by a command to set a new default role that has appropriate permissions, or no default role:

SET DEFAULT ROLE NONE FOR  test_user ;
SET DEFAULT ROLE  ROLE_NAME  for  test_user ;

To disable LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE, run the following command as the database administrator (shown here as root).
Disable local_infile for the running server:
SET GLOBAL local_infile = OFF;
Configure the server startup option to make the change persistent across server restarts:
[mariadb] local_infile=OFF
Restart the MariaDB Enterprise server, if required, for the configuration change to take effect.
If local_infile must remain enabled to support operational requirements, then ensure only authorized users have the FILE privilege.
Review database accounts:
SELECT USER, HOST FROM mysql.global_priv;
Review the assigned privileges for each account returned:
SHOW GRANTS FOR 'user'@'host';
If an unauthorized account has the FILE privilege, revoke it:
REVOKE FILE ON *.* FROM 'user'@'host';
If an unauthorized account has the GRANT OPTION privilege, revoke the unauthorized privilege or revoke the privileges that include GRANT OPTION.
Review the privileges assigned to each role:
SHOW GRANTS FOR role_name;
Revoke the FILE privilege from any role that is not authorized to perform file operations.
Verify the following after making the changes:
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'local_infile';
Returns only authorized users or roles retain the FILE privilege.

check.check-content:
To view the currently defined, port, as the DBA (root), run:

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES like 'port' ;

If the currently defined port is deemed prohibited, this is a finding.

Rule.fixtext:
Configure MariaDB settings to uniquely identify and authenticate all organizational users who log on/connect to the system.

To create roles, run:

CREATE ROLE app_read_role [options];

check.check-content:
Verify SSL is in use. 

Run the following database command: 

MariaDB> STATUS; 

Verify the line which starts with "SSL:" is as expected. It should return "cert is OK".

If the line returned is "Not in use", this is a finding.

Use results of next query to show SSL on for all users:

SELECT user,  host, ssl_type from mysql.user;

If "ssl_type" is blank, this is a finding.

Verify Transport Layer Security (TLS) configuration:

SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'tls_version';

The "tls_version" variable should show TLSv1.2,TLSv1.3. If not, this is a finding. 

SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'have_ssl';

The "have_ssl" variable should return "YES". If not, this is a finding.

As the DBA (root), verify the following settings: 

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'ssl_ca';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'ssl_cert';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'ssl_key';

Example location:
/etc/my.cnf.d/certs/

ls -l /etc/my.cnf.d/certs/

If the "owner:group" is not the database owner (mysql:mysql), this is a finding.

If permissions are not "600", this is a finding.

Query all users to confirm the issuer and subject are configured correctly: 

MariaDB>SELECT user, host, ssl_type, CAST(x509_issuer AS CHAR) AS issuer, CAST(x509_subject AS CHAR) AS subject FROM mysql.user;

If users are not mapped correctly, this is a finding.

Rule.fixtext:
Configure MariaDB Enterprise to require individual user authentication and prohibit direct use of shared accounts.

Ensure all users are assigned unique database accounts:

SELECT User, Host FROM mysql.user;

Remove or disable shared or generic accounts:

DROP USER '<shared_user>'@'<host>';

Configure authentication to require individual credentials for each user:

ALTER USER '<username>'@'<host>' IDENTIFIED BY '<strong_password>';

Ensure anonymous accounts are removed:

DELETE FROM mysql.user WHERE User='';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;

Configure access to shared resources through role-based access control rather than shared credentials:

CREATE ROLE '<role_name>';
GRANT <privileges> ON <database>.* TO '<role_name>';
GRANT '<role_name>' TO '<username>'@'<host>';
SET DEFAULT ROLE '<role_name>' TO '<username>'@'<host>';

Configure the system to prohibit credential sharing and require users to authenticate with their individually assigned accounts prior to accessing shared databases or resources.

check.check-content:
As the DBA (root), run:

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'tcp_keepalive_interval';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'tcp_keepalive_probes';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'tcp_keepalive_time';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'tcp_nodelay';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'max_statement_time';

If these settings are not configured, this is a finding.

Determine if MariaDB is configured to require SSL.

MariaDB> SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'require_secure_transport';

If "require_secure_transport" is not "ON", this is a finding.

If the application owner and Authorizing Official (AO) have determined that encryption of data at rest is not required, this is not a finding.

If full-disk encryption is being used, and the DBA or system administrator (SA) can demonstrate the use of disk-level encryption, this is not a finding.

One possible way to encrypt data within MariaDB is to use the file key management plugin. To determine if this is installed, check the following variables as the DBA:

SHOW PLUGINS; 

Confirm the "file_key_management.so" is listed.

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'file_key%';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'aria_en%';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'innodb_encrypt_tables';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'innodb_encrypt_log';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'innodb_tablespace%';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'encrypt%';

Confirm these are set to "ON".

If this is required and is not found, this is a finding. 

If controls do not exist or are not enabled, this is a finding.

Review MariaDB source code (stored procedures, functions, triggers) and application source code, to identify cases of dynamic code execution. 

Log in to MariaDB as the DBA and run the following for each database: 

SHOW FUNCTION STATUS;
SHOW PROCEDURE STATUS;
SHOW TRIGGERS FROM <database_name>;

Any user input should be handled through prepared statements.

If dynamic code execution is employed in circumstances where the objective could practically be satisfied by static execution with strongly typed parameters, this is a finding.

Log in to MariaDB and run as the DBA:

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'interactive_timeout';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'wait_timeout';

Review the configured timeout values.

If granular timeout controls for idle transactions are required, run:

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'idle_transaction_timeout';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'idle_write_transaction_timeout';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'idle_readonly_transaction_timeout';

If idle session termination is required and the timeout values exceed the approved inactivity threshold or are disabled, this is a finding.

Rule.fixtext:
Implement the organization's DAC policy in the security configuration of the database and DBMS, and, if applicable, the security configuration of the application(s) using the database.

To grant and revoke privileges as the DBA (root), use the following SQL syntax:

To grant user and role privileges:

GRANT  privilege  ON  database . table  TO  user|role ;

To revoke user and role privileges:

REVOKE  privilege_type  ON   database . table  FROM  user|role ;

Determine where, when, how, and by which principals or subjects elevated privileges are required.

MariaDB functions and stored procedures support the "SQL SECURITY" attribute, which determines whether a routine executes using the privileges of the invoker or the definer.

To modify the "SECURITY_TYPE" of a function or stored procedure, run:

ALTER FUNCTION function_name SQL SECURITY INVOKER;
or
ALTER FUNCTION function_name SQL SECURITY DEFINER;

For stored procedures, run:

ALTER PROCEDURE procedure_name SQL SECURITY INVOKER;
or
ALTER PROCEDURE procedure_name SQL SECURITY DEFINER;

"SQL SECURITY INVOKER" causes the routine to execute using the privileges of the user invoking the routine. "SQL SECURITY DEFINER" causes the routine to execute using the privileges of the account defined as the definer of the routine.

To review the current definition of a function or procedure, execute the following command as a DBA:

SHOW CREATE FUNCTION function_name;
or
SHOW CREATE PROCEDURE procedure_name;

If the definer account must be changed, the function or procedure must be recreated with the appropriate definer account.

Remove the existing routine if modification of the definer, if required:

DROP FUNCTION function_name;
DROP PROCEDURE procedure_name;

Recreate the function or procedure using the definition returned by the "SHOW CREATE" statement, specifying the appropriate "DEFINER" account in the "CREATE" statement. This ensures that elevated privileges are granted only where required and are executed under the appropriate security context.

check.check-content:
Review the security guide to determine whether the organization has defined the information at rest that is to be protected from disclosure, which must include, at a minimum, PII and classified information.

If the documentation indicates no information requires such protections, this is not a finding.

Review the configuration of MariaDB, operating system/file system, and additional software as relevant.

One possible way to encrypt data within MariaDB is to use the file key management plugin. To determine if this is installed, check the following variables as the DBA:

SHOW PLUGINS; 

Confirm the "file_key_management.so" is listed.

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'file_key%';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'aria_en%';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'innodb_encrypt_tables';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'innodb_encrypt_log';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'innodb_tablespace%';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'encrypt%';

Confirm these are "ON".

If this is required and is not found, this is a finding. 

If any of the information defined as requiring protection is not encrypted in a manner that provides the required level of protection and is not physically secured to the required level, this is a finding.

If the data owner does not have a strict requirement for ensuring data integrity and confidentiality is maintained at every step of the data transfer and handling process, this is not a finding.

As the DBA (shown here as root), verify the following settings: 

SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'ssl_ca';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'ssl_cert';
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'ssl_key'; 

If SSL is not enabled, this is a finding.

If MariaDB does not employ protective measures against unauthorized disclosure and modification during preparation for transmission, this is a finding.

Rule.fixtext:
Configure password expiration to require periodic password changes:

ALTER USER 'someusername'@'localhost' PASSWORD EXPIRE INTERVAL 60 DAY;

To view information about a user's password expiration settings, run:

SHOW CREATE USER 'foo4'@'test';

Account-Specific Policy: Password expiration can be configured for individual accounts using "ALTER USER" or "CREATE USER" with the "PASSWORD EXPIRE" option.

If a user's password expires and it is not changed, server behavior depends on the "disconnect_on_expired_password" system variable and whether the client supports expired passwords. In this case, the account is placed in a restricted mode allowing only password reset operations.

User accounts can also be manually disabled using the account lock command:

ALTER USER 'inactive_user'@'localhost' ACCOUNT LOCK;

MariaDB also provides an audit plugin that can log user activity such as logins, executed queries, and database modifications. This can assist administrators in identifying inactive accounts.

If an account is determined to be inactive, lock the account:

ALTER USER 'inactive_user'@'localhost' ACCOUNT LOCK;

Remove the account if it is no longer required:

DROP USER 'user'@'host';

If organizational policy requires the account to be retained, disable the account.

Verify that only authorized user and service accounts remain:

SELECT User, Host
FROM mysql.global_priv;

Configure MariaDB Enterprise to require users to change their password upon account recovery by enforcing password expiration.

Set password lifetime to enforce expiration:

Edit the mariadb-enterprise.cnf configuration file located in "/etc/my.cnf.d/".

Under [mariadb], add/edit the following: 

[mariadb]

default_password_lifetime = <organization-defined-value>;

Configure recovered or reset accounts to require immediate password change:

ALTER USER '<username>'@'<host>' PASSWORD EXPIRE;

Ensure administrative password reset procedures include setting the account to expired status:

ALTER USER '<username>'@'<host>' IDENTIFIED BY '<temporary_password>' PASSWORD EXPIRE;

Verify that accounts require password change upon next authentication:

SELECT User, Host, password_expired FROM mysql.user;

Ensure all password-based accounts subject to recovery processes are configured to require immediate password change upon first login following password reset.
